  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12(0)+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4\\,[{y}^{2}+2y]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y+1)^{2}-1]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}-4+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/assets/efaa5f474c5f505f6a1ca98469a77f8d21e059adee99c60cb9a2a7cb87600b5f.png)",
